WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.080
When Blade Runner came out in 1982, it left
fans speechless — but it also left them

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.149
with a ton of unanswered questions.

00:00:07.149 --> 00:00:11.200
Thirty-five years later, some of those questions
have finally been answered thanks to Blade

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.310
Runner 2049.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:13.580
That's the good news.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:14.580
The bad news?

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:18.600
Blade Runner 2049 has left us with even more
mysteries than before.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.970
Here's a spoiler-filled look at the biggest
unanswered questions in Blade Runner 2049.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:24.410
"What do you want?"

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:27.539
"I want to ask you some questions."

00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:29.939
So… is Deckard a replicant?

00:00:29.939 --> 00:00:34.350
Perhaps the biggest question from the original
Blade Runner is whether or not Harrison Ford's

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:37.870
character, the replicant-hunter Deckard, is
himself a replicant.

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:41.740
Though the film suggested that he was, it
was intentionally left ambiguous.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:46.900
Blade Runner 2049 takes that ambiguity and
uses it to totally troll the audience, with

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:51.520
Jared Leto's replicant magnate Wallace delivering
a whole monologue about whether or not Deckard

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.429
is a replicant — without actually answering
the question.

00:00:54.429 --> 00:00:58.079
This is one mystery it looks like we'll never
learn the answer to.

00:00:58.079 --> 00:00:59.519
Did K die?

00:00:59.519 --> 00:01:03.730
At the end of the film, K lays down in the
snow to die in a beautiful scene clearly meant

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:07.950
as a callback to the death of Rutger Hauer's
replicant leader Roy Batty in the original

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:08.950
Blade Runner.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:13.189
There's one key difference, however: we see
Roy die on-camera, while K's fate is left

00:01:13.189 --> 00:01:15.210
poetically and lyrically up in the air.

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:16.530
Sure, he probably dies.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:21.290
But the Blade Runner films love leaving things
open to interpretation, and K's "death" scene

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:23.729
is just another prime example.

00:01:23.729 --> 00:01:24.750
What's the deal with Joi?

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:30.399
One of the most enigmatic characters in the
movie is Joi, K's computerized virtual girlfriend.

00:01:30.399 --> 00:01:35.250
It's unclear whether she's a true AI, or just
a sophisticated program designed to emulate

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.460
human emotions.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:37.790
Does she really love K?

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:39.900
Or is she just programmed to pretend?

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:44.259
Knowing the truth may answer the other big
question about Joi: how and why did she end

00:01:44.259 --> 00:01:46.950
up bringing in Mariette for that super weird
love scene?

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:51.189
It seems beyond coincidence that Joi happens
to contact the one replicant specifically

00:01:51.189 --> 00:01:56.830
tasked with spying on K. If Joi truly loves
K, then the explanation she gives about wanting

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:59.740
to experience physical romance with K may
be true.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:04.320
But if she's just a program, you have to wonder
who programmed her to help the replicant rebellion.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:05.969
Could she have been hacked by them?

00:02:05.969 --> 00:02:10.420
There's more to Joi than meets the eye, but
exactly what is anyone's guess.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:12.520
Who put Ana's memories in K?

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:17.010
The entire plot hinges on the fact that K
has memories leading him to believe he's actually

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:18.010
Deckard's son.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:22.099
However, it turns out that while they're real
memories, they actually belong to Deckard's

00:02:22.099 --> 00:02:26.980
daughter, Dr. Ana Stelline, who specializes
in creating false memories for replicants.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:31.210
Considering she tells K that putting real
memories into replicants is illegal, it begs

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:35.840
the question of why her memories were recorded
in the first place, not to mention how her

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.790
memories happened to end up in the exact replicant
tasked with tracking her down.

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:43.519
Perhaps if we knew how K ended up on this
case in the first place we'd have a better

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:44.519
idea.

00:02:44.519 --> 00:02:48.371
But when the film opens, K is already tracking
down the replicant farmer Sapper Morton, who

00:02:48.371 --> 00:02:50.020
holds the key to the mystery.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.099
Who told K about Sapper, and why?

00:02:52.099 --> 00:02:53.819
Coincidence, or part of a larger plot?

00:02:53.819 --> 00:02:57.610
The more questions you ask about it, the more
new questions it creates.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.540
Did Ana know Deckard was her father?

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:01.670
So, about those memories Ana has.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:05.470
She tells K that her parents were ready to
go offworld and put her in this bubble when

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:09.319
she got sick at the age of eight to protect
her from the crappy world outside.

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:12.790
That's clearly a lie, though, because she
has memories — which have been implanted

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:15.550
in K — about her time living in the orphanage.

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:18.599
So what does she really remember, and what
does she really know about herself?

00:03:18.599 --> 00:03:23.310
The replicant rebel leader Freysa, played
by Hiam Abass, tells K she has Deckard's daughter

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:24.660
safely hidden away.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:28.709
If she's telling the truth, it implies Ana
knows about the rebellion and her own replicant

00:03:28.709 --> 00:03:30.530
parentage, and is in on the whole thing.

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:32.379
But is Freysa telling the truth?

00:03:32.379 --> 00:03:34.659
Did Ana know Deckard and Rachael were her
parents?

00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:38.360
If the movie had gone on for another ten seconds
we would have gotten the answer, but since

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:41.459
it didn't, we'll have to settle for just guessing.

00:03:41.459 --> 00:03:43.110
What happens next?

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:47.340
At the end of the movie, humanity seems trapped
between two different replicant factions.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:51.480
On one hand, Wallace wants to build replicants
who can reproduce, allowing them to spread

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:52.770
across the galaxy.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:56.709
On the other hand, the band of rebels responsible
for hiding Deckard's daughter want to rise

00:03:56.709 --> 00:03:58.060
up and overthrow their masters.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:02.930
If either of these factions prevail, humanity
has some pretty nasty times ahead regardless.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:04.240
But what does happen?

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:08.909
Wallace is still alive with all the resources
of the solar system at his disposal, the rebels

00:04:08.909 --> 00:04:12.750
are off somewhere making new eyepatches for
their leader, and the whole powderkeg seems

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.659
about to blow when the film cuts to black.

00:04:14.659 --> 00:04:18.320
Looks like we'll have to wait for Blade Runner
2079 to find out!

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:19.320
Thanks for watching!

